483 0 obj
<<
/Title (Are all genetic disorders inherited in the same way?)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 481 0 R
/Next 487 0 R
>>
endobj
484 0 obj
<<
/Title (X-linked inheritance: -)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 488 0 R
/Next 482 0 R
>>
endobj
485 0 obj
<<
/Title (There are several reasons. Family members may have been carriers. A recessive gene change can pass quietly through several generations. People may carry it without knowing. Older relatives may have had symptoms but never received a correct diagnosis. Some people may be mildly affected while others have more serious symptoms.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 482 0 R
/Next 486 0 R
>>
endobj
486 0 obj
<<
/Title (Biological relatives share some of the same genes because they have common ancestors. This means relatives are more likely than two completely unrelated people to carry some of the same gene changes. This becomes important with recessive genetic disorders. If a recessive gene change is already present in a family, two relatives may both carry it without knowing. If they have a child together, there is a greater chance that the child could inherit the changed gene from both sides of the family. However, there are several important things to understand. Cousin marriage does not create genetic disorders. The gene change was already present in the family. Being biologically related simply increases the chance that both parents may carry the same recessive gene change. Not every child of related parents will have a genetic disorder. Many children of related parents are healthy. Unrelated parents can also both be carriers. This is especially important for conditions that are common in a population, such as beta-thalassaemia in Pakistan. Relationship alone cannot tell you your exact risk. Knowing that two people are cousins cannot tell us whether they actually carry the same disease-causing gene. Testing and family history are much more useful for understanding the risk of a particular condition.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 482 0 R
/Prev 485 0 R
>>
endobj
